Journal, Phase 3

Look at the constructor for your subsystem classes. From the outside, can a developer or a unit test tell that these classes depend on ConfigurationManager?
What are hidden dependencies, and why do they make unit testing individual classes in isolation more challenging?


No, a developer or unit test cannot tell from the subsystem constructor that it depends on ConfigurationManager because the dependency is accessed inside the methods instead.
This is called a hidden dependency because the class relies on another class without showing it in the constructor or method parameters. 
Hidden dependencies can make unit testing harder because the test cannot easily provide a different ConfigurationManager or mock version of it.




